\f0\i0\fs28 \cf2 Maya pointed at the giant creature stirring in the park\'92s lake, as the other kids\'92 chanting flooded the air:\
\'93Monster Maya! Monster Maya! Monster Maya!\'94\
Maybe she was. If she could see things that others couldn\'92t, live in a world different than theirs, wasn\'92t she something alien? The thought slammed into her 6-year-old mind. \
Her parents jumped from the park benches when they saw the wild bob of ginger curls and teary, azure eyes running towards them. An exchange of knowing glances between the two. \
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 It had happened again?
\f0\i0  \

\f1\i What terrible thing had she seen this time?
\f0\i0  \
They didn\'92t understand their daughter\'92s imagination but had hoped other kids would share it and partake. At least that\'92s what the therapist suggested. Anything to mitigate the development of antisocial behaviors.\uc0\u8232 \
Day turned to night. Windows painted Maya\'92s room with twilight\'92s moon as she lay on the floor, crayons scattered around her. She was supposed to be asleep by this time, but had been too afraid. Afraid, like many children, of monsters hidden in the closet or under the bed. But, for Maya, it was reality. Doodling was her means of escape.\
However, hands grow tired.\
Once hers ached enough, she finally ceased resisting and looked up. Her eyes traced the secrets of her room\'92s dark corners. They saw shapes, some static and others morphing, resembling things from this earth but just not\'85quite. Some loomed tall, others crept and slithered. Each had its own distinctive features. She was tired of being afraid. She gazed upon the things, looked at them for more than just a few seconds.\
One was a limb with a cut, starting as bright red ooze, quickly drying and purpling, until it charred into black crusts. Whenever she looked away from it and back, the thing would revert to its original form and start the process again. It reminded her of the bruise she received at the playground that day.\
Another: a cloud of darkness, startling her with a thunderclap whenever she tried looking away. Maya noticed it had first appeared when an earlier storm subsided.\
Next: a cyclopean, reptilian eye. A crimson slit on a circular bed of bile, never ceasing to stare at her, no matter how many times she turned and looked back at it. It always stared. Maya couldn\'92t discern when this demon had appeared.\
They, and many others, had been accompanying her everywhere and for so long. Soccer practice? Birthday parties? Even the bathroom? It didn\'92t matter. For 2 years they had been following her, on and off. And HOW they followed her. They seemed to get bigger from her fear, from her attention. So she tried depriving them of it, but to no avail. They were still there, creeping in and out of her life. She wanted, so desperately, to be free of them. \
Perhaps her avoidance infuriated them? She thought of this as her hand continued to ache from her escapist drawing. Her eyes and mind had grown weary from her prison of horrors. This was the first time she was just tired enough to face them unflinchingly.\
And then a thought. If they wanted attention, she would give it to them. She pushed through the fear and the aching, and did the thing that she did best: draw. But this time with purpose. She would give the things a taste of their own visage.\
She looked to and from the cut limb as her hands worked. Her final illustration: red ooze on the limb\'92s upper half, purple in the middle, and crusty black at the bottom. And when she was done, when she thought she had perfectly captured the thing, she took her drawing and presented it to the creature. But it was gone.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 What a butt
\f0\i0 , the 6-year-old thought. She had worked hard on the piece and her intended audience just vanished? It wasn\'92t until the next time she tried drawing a creature that she realized what had actually happened. Maya didn\'92t understand it, but this was her first victory over the monsters.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Whispers. Indiscernible little rumblings with the occasional spike. Maya heard them crashing onto the gallery walls from the outside like ocean waves hitting some shore-bound structure. \
They came from the crowd of soon-to-be gallery perusers. Among them: family members, faculty, alumni, other students, and anyone who passed by an advertisement thinking it would be a fun way to spend their Saturday afternoon. \
\'93Hear that outside?\'94 The Dean of the College of Arts exclaimed, \'93Among the crowd out there could be the means of your future!\'94\
And that\'92s what this was really about, at least for those whose work was on display. Scouts from the country\'92s best graduate art programs came to these kinds of senior undergraduate exhibits. They came to asses talent, window shopping prospective students for their institutes and conservatories. Maya thought of their low acceptance rates. Never had teen numbers scared her so.\
She scanned the room for the usual crop of star students, probing for signs of nervousness. Maya\'92s exhibit had been set up between those of her friends, the Barovich twins. To her right: Jade was coiling her dyed-green locks, her usual defense technique, making it indiscernible whether she was fidgety or flirting. To her left: Jack had his hands clasped behind his back, flannel-covered chest and goateed chin up, as if bracing for an incoming wave. Across the gallery, Isabelle scratched at her sun-spotted arm like she was digging it for gold. Harrison was in the gallery\'92s Northeast corner, doing the same scanning as Maya. His dark eyes met hers and she quickly looked away.\
It had been since Highschool, three years ago, that Maya last identified as the \'93star artist.\'94 North East Bard\'92s College collected such identities. This initially allured Maya but now made her feel small. She mentally, and sarcastically, thanked the Dean for reminding her of the pressure.\
\'93If nothing comes of this, remember that you still have your final! Resilience is a necessary trait of any creator!\'94\
Another pang of angst. Maya hadn\'92t started the project they were given three weeks ago.\
The two sets of double doors on each side of the gallery burst open, a pressure finally released. A tide of whispers flooded the exhibition floor as attendees poured in from both sides. Maya could hear the 
\f1\i pitter-patter
\f0\i0  of their approach. She would be the last to receive them as she and the Barovich twins were in the middle of the gallery. The ocean of people and whispers became louder and louder until they were close.\
They approached Jade\'92s wire sculptures first. Jade\'92s world was a world of aluminum: aluminum people, aluminum animals, and aluminum furniture. Maya admired how she could reconstruct life with such a base material, showcasing its skeletal elements without the maskings of color or texture. And the whispers poured over the aluminum skeletons, granting them flesh and life with their interpretations. These exhibitions were a level of vulnerability that seemed to make Jade uncomfortable, an artist\'92s skinny dip. She seemed to want to coil both sides of her hair, but restrained herself from doing so.\
Then the whispers went for Jack. Jack\'92s world was one of beautiful utility. Bowls and pans and utensils and a chair. One would think he was moving into his section of the gallery. But the bowls had glass birds and ceramic flowers flowing from their brims. The chair was a velvety red V, a heart held together by lingerie-lace-like wiring. Jack\'92s exhibit was one where perusers were allowed, even welcomed, to touch. And so the whispers drowned Jack and his work.\
And then the tides came for Maya. They started washing in slowly, meandering and stopping and then moving again. They came until they filled Maya\'92s little shop of horrors: her ink drawings and paintings of creepy disturbances.\
There was the doctor, whose grin was devilish and all too excited. A flatline from an electrocardiogram served as the line that separated his upper, yellow-stained teeth from his lower, blackened ones. And he held a clipboard, turning it towards the viewer. But the \'93clipboard\'94 was only a frame of a clipboard. Rather than pages, the clipboard frame held a mirror, cracked and marred with blood.\
And there was the old cut limb. Maya drew it even when the limb beast wasn\'92t around. To her, it represented her power. Both because it was the first time she dispelled a monster, but also because of how far she had come. She drew the limb repeatedly because capturing the subtle flakes of skin-texture demanded practice. And her practice had paid off. Once drawn in simple crayon, the ink-lined limb now looked so real that she was afraid someone would mistake her for some trophy-collecting serial killer.\
\'93Incredible! You are so talented.\'94\
\'93I\'92m speechless right now.\'94\
\'93I bet you could make insane Halloween decorations.\'94\
Maya wondered if that last comment reflected a lack of \'93seriousness\'94 people viewed her art with. Regardless, the few people that approached Maya were usually complimentary, their words like little islands of positivity. But it was the whispers, the ocean of potential criticisms, that soaked Maya\'92s mind. What invalidating thing would she find in its depths? \
Maya looked around the gallery. Perhaps the number of people staying in one location would be a sign of whose work was more \'93popular.\'94 Would that somehow denote who the scouts would be more interested in? Did the scouts pick students who drew more crowds of people?\
Maya shook this off. She hated that her feelings about art had come to this: measuring and scaling, comparing their value. She looked to her own work and remembered when it made her feel great to express her demons and show them to others. How other students gazed in awe. Maybe that was what she missed. How special she had once been.\
Once the exhibition was over, curators started taking the students\'92 pieces back to storage for them. Maya\'92s doctor piece was being carried away as Harrison approached her. He took a passing glance at the piece and smirked.\
\'93How freaky\'85\'94 he grinned as he strode past her.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Cocky cunt
\f0\i0 , Maya thought.\
Harrison\'92s world was the \'93real\'94 world: a jumble of hair fibers, skin particles, wrinkles, sun spots, shadow gradients, and other mundane action captured in shouting, screaming sharpness. The dean made it painfully obvious that Harrison\'92s photo-realism was his favorite. The two walked alongside each other and out of the gallery like two love-struck teenagers.\
\'93Looking forward to seeing that final you were telling me about,\'94 Maya heard the dean say to him. And with that, the pressure was back on. Soon, students would hear from the schools whose scouts showed up. Maya could feel her heart racing.\
Finals. Scouts. The rest of the day was supposed to be time to unwind. The students walked out together, relieving each other with stories of mutual nervousness. Maya joined them, but something kept her distracted. Despite the leave of gallery perusers, Maya could still hear the whispers.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 She walked with her classmates out of the gallery, but she could catch their low rumble. The murmurs sat with her at the school\'92s lunch hall. Their airy echo followed her back to her dorm. And, when Maya was finally home, she waged her war against the monster of sound.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 It didn\'92t take 6-year-old Maya long to realize her power and wield it against her tormentors. \
At birthday parties, she would catch a glimpse of some decrepit thing, find a napkin, and erase its existence with the pen she now took everywhere.\
During a break at soccer practice, she drew on the grass with her foot, capturing some ghastly countenance on the field\'92s sidelines like a a green etch-a-sketch.\
Even in the bathroom, she sculpted whatever terrible life form lay before her using toilet paper. Maya aligned the paper sculpture with the silhouette of the monster, until the paper form blocked the real thing from her view. When she moved the paper version out of her line of sight, she found she had wiped the real thing from existence.\
Each drawing turned fear into art until fear itself became a thing Maya had chained to the canvas. The monsters never stopped coming to her over the years. And she never stopped vanquishing them. Her walls became a gallery of paper triumphs, a collection of creature trophies.\
And Maya changed too. The creatures had once made her feel like a monster herself. They kept her at bay. But she was not a monster, she was a monster killer. What else could she accomplish? \
And with that mindset, art became identity. She prided herself when the other girls asked her to decorate the covers of their notebooks with elaborate illustrations. \'93Girl, I got you\'94 was her response when her best friend was ghosted by a tall tangle of white lankiness named Kevin. Maya drew him. His limbs as wispy as the ends of a ghost, his skin as opaque as any apparition she had ever seen. She highlighted his worst features with ink but left his best in pencil. And, as a member of the school\'92s yearbook team, made sure that ghastly Kevin was imprinted on the school\'92s tome forever. \
Maya\'92s parents went from concerned about her lack of social life to annoyed by it. Their daughter snuck out. She stayed out late and kept the company of kids who found themselves in all sorts of trouble. She dawned dark shadows and dressed like it was Halloween, only she did it all year round. But they couldn\'92t be too worried. Maya\'92s grades were excellent. And they had to be because Maya had her eyes set on IFA. \
The Institute of Fine Arts was the alma mater for many of Maya\'92s favorite artists. It was a garden that sprouted revolutionary creators, smelled of depth and soul, bloomed wonders in winter and spring semesters. And the prestige of that garden would serve as the peak testament to her power.\
But IFA was a garden of GRADUATE wonders. Their main harvesting ground for undergraduates was North East Bard\'92s College. Maya knew this and so she worked rigorously on her craft to get in.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 She saw how people were like her monsters, wanting to be seen and understood. How they haunted each other in their pursuit of this goal. And so she drew them. And nature was like her monsters, offering captivating features in an effort to not be forgotten. She drew nature too. She drew it all until her and drawing were the same, until North East Bard\'92s College accepted her as an undergraduate art major.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 From her command room dormitory, Maya launched her assault on the whispers. All that did, however, was messy her room. Pages littered her floor, bullet shell casings of ink and color, fallen paper darts that missed their board.\
On some pages, she had drawn an ear with lines marking the sound waves entering them. Other pages contained crowds of people, sometimes even the very crowd from the exhibit. One page had nothing, reflecting how sound itself had no image that could be captured.\
But it didn\'92t matter how elaborate or simplistic the drawing. The whispers remained. Never had background noise been such an asshole. The crowd of sounds taunted her with the rise and fall of their volume. When Maya occasionally looked around at her fallen paper soldiers, she realized how none of them were an attempt at her upcoming final. And her anxiety somehow made the whispers even louder.\
Maya tried to listen to them, but couldn\'92t make out a sound. She took a deep breath and sighed in frustration. That\'92s when she heard: \'93weird.\'94\
The lone word plopped out of the ocean of whispers and returned just as quickly. She listened to the white noise in search of where it fell but found nothing. \
Luckily, the noise wasn\'92t always there. Maya would occasionally catch a break as she distracted herself with her phone, TV, or reading a book. But the second she even thought about that upcoming final, their low reverberation returned.\
More than the noise, what bothered Maya was what the noise meant. She had grown so accustomed to conquering the things that plagued her. Her time at North East Bard\'92s College chipped away at that confidence. And now, with the pressures of the senior exhibitions and final, she felt how she hadn\'92t felt in a long time: helpless. Helpless like that little 6-year-old her.\
A text notification from Jade broke her rumination.\
\'93Come to the SLOTH, NOW!\'94\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Maya expected what was coming. She just hadn\'92t expected it to be so soon. Her heart raced and her mood fell to some gravitational pull. The whispers seemed to grow in their volume at this. Maya would have to continue her war later. She packed her things, fixed herself up, and headed over to where every art undergraduate was bound to be now: the SLOTH.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2  The Student Lounge On The Hill used to be the school's lunch hall before that function moved to another building. It now served as a space where students could come together, for work or play. Being on the summit of a hill, the climb made one feel as if they had to work for the space\'92s homey log cabin walls.\
When Maya entered, 5 students stood among a 20-person crowd.\
\'93We look forward to speaking with you further about a potential residency in our conservatory,\'94 one of the 5 read off her laptop. And the student crowd cheered for her.\
Maya\'92s heart sank when, among the 5, was Jack.\uc0\u8232 \
Jack: whose utilitarian art had started as his taking furniture and painting them. Jack: who gazed upon every work that Maya had ever produced with awe and praise and wishes that he could become as skillful as her. Jack: who, much to Maya\'92s quiet shame, had served as her ego\'92s bedrock in this college art world that made her feel so ordinary.\uc0\u8232 \
And never had she felt as ordinary as when she watched the 5 of those students reading out their scout emails. In each undergraduate class, only an average of 7 or 8 students made it into graduate arts programs. 5 of those spots had just been taken.\
Isabelle was the next fortunate student to be reading her scout email in front of the entire grade. In Isabelle\'92s world, paint met reflection, literally. She glued mirror glass shards onto a painted canvas, making the viewer part of the artwork. One of her best pieces had been of her dog. The mirror shards separated the canine into pieces. The head was a puppy but its leg and paws were much longer with overgrown hair. Even the mirror shards had some painting on them: tennis balls and dog treats. The viewer could see the remnants of memories, between owner and pet, in every shard.\
Isabelle\'92s mirror dog reminded Maya of her own mirror doctor. What might scouts have thought of her doctor?\
Now Maya was trying to make out what Isabelle was saying but the whispers had grown so loud she could hardly hear anything else. And then:\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 \'93I don\'92t get it\'85\'94
\f0\i0 \
Another lone, whisper sound bite. \
She was still thinking of her doctor painting. And, without being fully conscious of it, she applied the words to her thoughts. \
Perhaps her doctor piece was too on the nose? It wasn\'92t exactly a subtle piece. Isabelle\'92s works always left enough room for different interpretations.\
Maya felt something give a slight push on her shoulder. It was Jade.\
\'93I know\'85I feel the same way.\'94 Jade began, \'93Like I\'92m so happy for them but also sad for me and everyone else who didn\'92t get the same recognition. Fuck this.\'94 \
\'93Yeah, art shouldn\'92t be things you weigh on a scale and say one thing weighs more than the other,\'94 Maya responded.\
\'93Exactlyyyy.\'94 Something about the way she trailed her word reassured Maya.\
But as Maya gazed back upon the 5 standing students, it hit her just how much her art was, in fact, being weighed. And her creative weight had apparently been light. And the whispers were like nausea incarnated into sound. Their unceasing grumble only stressed her unease. \
\'93So how far along you at with your final?\'94\
Panic. Whispers. Their volume rose as she pushed away the knowledge that she hadn\'92t even begun. And they were just 2 weeks out now.\
\'93Oh, like halfway through\'94 her lie reached out over the loud, whisper roar.\
The 5 students were finally done presenting their scout emails. Notably, Harrison was not among them. Jack instantly returned to Jade:\
\'93Alright sis, now we\'92re going to kick some ass on your final. You\'92ll be the next one up there.\'94\
Jade smiled and hugged her brother. Jack caught Maya\'92s eyes over his sister\'92s shoulder.\
\'93And you! You\'92re not allowed to feel bad either. Fight ain\'92t over for you.\'94\
Maya mouthed a thank you and smiled at him. Despite the hit to her ego, if anyone else would hear from a scout she wanted it to be Jack.\uc0\u8232 \
\'93Yeah Maya, I\'92m sure some Haunted House will come to the next exhibit.\'94\
Harrison had snuck up to their group.\
Maya rolled her eyes. Harrison had been relentless ever since Yayoi Kusama visited the college and remarked on Maya\'92s works, but not his.\
\'93Wow. So thoughtful of you to say, Harry. Maybe if they need a dick, they\'92ll add you to the House.\'94 Maya shot back, knowing Harrison hated being called Harry.\
He rolled his eyes. \'93You don\'92t even belong here.\'94\
\'93What the fuck did you just say?\'94 Jade spat.\
\'93Oh, come on, you know it\'92s true. Her stuff may be cute at a Renaissance fair, but it\'92s not ADULT art. It\'92s fucking juvenile.\'94\
Maya resented that his words bothered her for even a moment. But \'93juvenile\'94 lingered in consciousness like a sour smell.\
She thought of her 3 years at North East Bard\'92s college. The reactions and suggestions of professors asking her to make the monsters more \'93real.\'94 Her initial college paintings had been so fantastical. She crafted wrinkled witches with hair made of spider webs and labyrinths of veins, athlete werewolves drinking Gatorade bottles with a Full Moon \'93G,\'94 a pile of dreadlocks with eyes barely poking out of them and a small fissure that allowed for the smoking of a bong. But North East Bard\'92s College was a \'93serious\'94 institution that demanded \'93serious\'94 work. Through its pressure and its shame, it chiseled away any spark of silliness from Maya\'92s monsters. Now, they were no longer\'85\

\f1\i Juvenile
\f0\i0 \
She heard the word again, but this time in the whispers. Their volume had muted the argument in front of her between Harrison and Jade. Maya could still gather the gist of their interaction when Jade pointed to his nether regions with one hand while holding up a pinky with the other.\
The whispers dispersed when Maya laughed.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93How\'92s that for \'91juvenile?\'92\'94 Jade\'92s voiced emerged from their exit.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93You do this to yourself.\'94 Jack shook his head as he examined Maya\'92s litany of punk rock posters.\
 She finally revealed the truth to Jade and him. Despite being a week from due date, she hadn\'92t even begun the final project.\
\'93What\'92s been stopping you?\'94 Jade inquired with her steely stare that could disarm the most guarded being.\
\'93I don\'92t know\'85\'94 Maya wished that would be good enough for them. \'93I don\'92t think I\'92ve ever been this nervous\'85\'94\
Jack and Jade exchanged a knowing glance.\
\'93Ok look\'85\'94 Jack started. \'93I\'92m going to say something that you might roll your eyes at me for. Might be easy for me to say. But hear me out\'85It DOESN\'92T matter if you get scouted.\'94\
Maya scoffed. \'93You\'92re right. That IS easy for you to say, Mr. One-of-the-lucky-5.\'94\
Jade interjected: \'93But he\'92s right, Maya. IFA, and the others, they\'92re just schools. We make art. You don\'92t need credentials for that. You don\'92t need Juliard to act or sing and you don\'92t need IFA to be a great artist.\'94\
Maya thought back to the artists she knew had gone to IFA, the sheer coincidence of their having gone to this same place. IFA sure FELT like a pre-requisite. \
\'93We\'92re going to help you choose your next monster,\'94 Jack added.\
\'93Girl, just draw Harrison with a stick up his ass.\'94 Jade threw in.\
Maya almost spit some coffee out of her mouth.\
\'93I SHOULD make that little shit my next project. He\'92s monstrous enough.\'94\
\'93God, I wish I saw his face when the 5 were up there.\'94 Jade leaned back in her chair. \'93He was probably holding back tears.\'94\
Maya wanted to throw more fuel on the Harrison-bashing fire, but realized that would make her a hypocrite. She too had looked enviously at the 5. Why wasn\'92t she chosen to be among them?\uc0\u8232 \
And then she was disgusted. She could actually relate to Harrison. She, too, knew that longing to be seen, to be validated.\
Over the corner of Jack\'92s shoulder, Maya could see the reptilian eye staring at her. She casually got her notebook and pencil to draw it while the Barovich twins brainstormed what other monsters Maya could use for her final. She had remembered something she had forgotten as she etched the vertical, narrow iris.\
People are like her monsters.\
Not just Harrison and his pompous attitude. EVERYONE. They just want to be seen, understood, validated. Every artist in their grade created art to express themselves, to capture a piece of themselves in their creations. They show their work to others, beckoning to be seen. They felt good when others appreciated their work. \
When did she forget this?\
She made the realization back in high school. Maybe the pressure of East Bard\'92s College had squeezed the knowledge out of her. Or maybe she had needed the mania of this pressure chamber to remember it again.\
Maya thought of the other students. If she drew monsters because she saw them everywhere and wanted to conquer them, why did the others choose their genres?\
Jack and his utility art. Jack WAS always trying to be useful. And Jade and her aluminum, displaying the world as it is underneath any pretense? Fitting for the girl with no filter. Isabelle and her mirrors? Maya thought back to the times she caught Isabelle journaling out on the yard in front of the SLOTH. Isabelle was cautious in her wording of things. She was as reflective as her mirror art.\
And Harrison? What did photo-realism express? \
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Freaky
\f0\i0 \

\f1\i Juvenile
\f0\i0 \
His words echoed in her mind. She thought of the wrinkles on his subjects\'92 foreheads. She thought of the vascularity of their forearm veins, the little bits of unibrow hair he left above the ridge of their freckled noses.\
Harrison\'92s world is a world of ugly truths, unflattering observations, magnified imperfections.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 After a few rounds of shooting down the Barovich twins\'92 monster ideas, she told them they had unintentionally given her the idea for her final. Not only that, but being with her friends also dispersed the whispers, at least for the time being. They left her to her work.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Crumbles of papers filled Maya\'92s dormitory floor. This time, they were not of the whispers but of her final. First step of any of her pieces was to sketch the thing that she intended to paint or ink. Her idea was simple: a humanoid creature clamoring for attention.\
But Maya usually only drew the monsters she had actually seen around her. Any time she attempted to draw a creature that she hadn\'92t actually seen, she found she lacked the interest to complete the work. She pushed through, regardless. There had been plenty of humanoid monsters in her past. And all of creatures clamor for attention. Not to mention, finishing her final and making it presentable to scouts should instigate her interest enough.\
She could imagine its posture: bent over as if begging. She could envision its gaze, looking to its surroundings for validation. \
But she couldn\'92t decide on what other qualities to give it. What physical features illustrate a desperation to belong?\
Maya was stuck. And that\'92s when the whispers came for her. Their wispy tones filled her ears. And she thought of how desperately she wished they\'92d leave her alone. And how helpless she was to make this so. They only grew louder with each desperate thought.\
The whispers flooded her, an orchestra of doubt. And the orchestra\'92s music WAS paralysis. She mourned the loss of that confident girl who slew her monsters. But how could she draw a sound? Despite having done so before, she looked around, trying to find the source of the whispers. Was she going mad?\
That\'92s when she saw the eye: the cyclopean, reptilian eye from that night when she was 6 and from behind Jack a few days prior. It hovered above her now, on the ceiling. Always staring. Always judging.\
And she drew it. \
It disappeared, but the whispers remained.\
\'93FUCK!!\'94 she threw her pencil.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 CRACK
\f0\i0  the pencil had hit her mirror, leaving it with veins of separation.\uc0\u8232 \
\'93Fuck\'85\'94 Maya whispered as she approached the cracked mirror. The shards made her think of Isabelle, her mirror art, her caution and self-reflection.\
A pause. Maya thought again of that confident girl who slayed her monsters. And she laughed, laughed because life could be such an ironic asshole. And also because Jack had been right after all. She DID do all this to herself. \
Maya thought she had been so clever in observing that people were like her monsters, clamoring to be seen and understood. Yet she missed the next progression of that thought.\
Maya took to a new sheet of paper. She drew the framework for her final. Maya didn\'92t need a reference for this one. She had been unknowingly preparing herself for this painting her entire life. She had viewed the thing plenty of times, but it wasn\'92t until now that she came to gaze upon it. To accept it for what it is.\
With her pencil, she outlined every fear she could think of, things she had always covered up or erased. She stomached through the discomfort, and with her pen, cemented their disgusting details onto the page. She forced herself to look at the thing, things really, that made her churn. She captured their likeness, forever, on the page.\
\pard\fi440\sl480\sa400\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And when she finished, there was finally silence.\
\pard\sl480\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf3 *\
\pard\fi440\sl480\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 2nd Exhibition day was here. Students, their galleries, the crowds outside. It was all happening again. And from inside the gallery, Maya could hear the rumbling from outside.\
But when the whispers entered this time, they were different. They were occasional, non-judgemental, and always came from an identifiable source.\
Not that Maya paid much attention to them. She resolved to enter the exhibit without applying pressure to herself. External validation was her enemy. She didn\'92t look off to the other students. She didn\'92t try to assess which attendee seemed \'93scout-like.\'94 Maya was simply\'85there.\
She let the occasional comments of good will flow past her without raising too much ego. She let the waves of whispers pass over, through her, as she floated above them. The few moments that she felt her anxiety rise, she took a deep breath and stared at her final:\
A wild bob of ginger curls flowed over azure eyes. The feeble thing was hunched over. Its eyes searched, begged, for approval. The body was technically, but not actually, naked. She covered some parts of the skin with splatters of paint and blots of ink. And, in lieu of gothic garments, the thing was dressed in the shadows of her monsters.\
Asheville Writer\'92s Group:\
The Good:\
Will: Good job on depicting hauntings. Arresting imagery. Read Dan Shawn for reference. His \'93The Bees\'94 and \'93Ill Will\'94 are masterclasses in slow burn hauntings, internal declines, and forbidding doom. Liked the ending. Title is a bit of a giveaway but thats not necessarily a problem. Strong choice to not see response of scouts and audience. Good job rendering the horror of cut throat academia and the arts. Good dialogue that felt believable. Very believable world.\
Sam: Fun premise. Not much fiction out there about artists feeling inadequate. Enjoyed the scan of the various artists and the calm before the storm. Love the crowd as whispers, the monstrous nature of hidden judgements. Really specific, terrifying monsters. Hard to imagine students presenting scout emails but it felt right. That painted the picture of how shallow and disgusting art school can be.\
Kelsey: Fascinating Premis, never seen it before. Lots of turns of phrase. Nice break of formality with casual talk (What a butt). Liked the ocean motif with the whispers. The use of twins was great given short length. Do a great job of being their own people while highlighting Maya\'92s journey. Not much dialogue but what we do have is pretty believable. That\'92s rare for short stories. Enjoyed distinction of monsters that she sees and something auditory. More room for bringing that out more. Fresh setting she doesn\'92t come across often.\
Michael: Very textural. The use of sound was complimentary. Strong sense of interiority done in the third person, which is hard to pull off. The third person POV has the right blend of objectivity and bias. Liked the idea of the 5. Strongest part of the story was the interiority / meeting someone that wants something they can\'92t have and puts the frustrations into art. Compelling world and character.\
Scott: Loved the idea that making art about what we have issues with. Ending was sweet. It felt fitting. She felt like an outcast and was able to come to accept herself. Liked the descriptions of the artists\'92 work. Maybe you could lean into it even more. Jack\'92s art seemed so inviting and confident despite his nervousness. \
\
The Bad:\
Will: Biggest critique is the piece feels light on detail. It manifests in different ways. Its most obvious because there\'92s such vividly to Maya\'92s imaginings. By contrast, there\'92s lots of stuff where the edges feel not drawn so clearly. Could be lots more descriptions of rooms to make a sense of place. In a piece that was so visual, he felt the setting lacked descriptions. Lack of detail also manifests in not feeling like we know Maya at all. It\'92s clear she suffers from insecurity in a universal relatable way. Aside from some hypocrisy, there aren\'92t much detectable flaws. Will doesn\'92t know what was happening to her at 6 years old that prompted this shift. That is pivotal to understanding her character. Her very early victory over the monsters feel unearned. He wanted the 6 year old to suffer first. This is where Dan Shaun can come in handy. The characters are deeply flawed and yet you empathize with them so much. Raise the stakes for both younger and present day Maya. We lose the thread of her at 6 and we can revisit her at 6 by the end. Consider a double climax with both past and present Maya. Right now, we have her only succeeding and then the occasional brush with a mild antagonist. He wants to see her suffer more so he can root for her more. Theres a slightly slippery slope with the premise of literal fears. They should not only affect her mentally but also have an effect on her physically. Bad example but you can have her come to class covered in slime. Something like that. Also play with an origin for the monsters. There\'92s gotta be something at 6 that started the monsters. Doesn\'92t have to be revealed at the beginning. Could be at the end. Nit-pick: Will is taken out of a piece with all caps. Italics are your friend. All caps and exclamation points make the reader feel less serious and more cartoonish. There\'92s a number of moments where there\'92s telling and not showing. Try to trust your reader 10% more. Make the artists fight in a more back-handed way. Also, lean deeper into the 5 people presenting their scout emails. Lean into the fraternity aspect of this college. Make it more cut-throat. Tease out more details in order for the reader to live in this world. Michael got Will thinking about the throwaway character of Kevin. Maya does quite a cruel thing with him and we never hear consequences of it. If there are consequences for the monsters, why not Maya? If Maya is the ultimate monster, have Maya be monstrous. Maybe she uses her power for evil to get her into the 5? Make her use the power for evil.\
Sam: Lacking those really specific details that help ground the reader into the world. Lot of exposition in parts. We could do with less of the narrator\'92s voice and more of Maya\'92s. Less statements about what people are thinking. It\'92s tough because there are a lot of logistics to explain. So its hard to get away from that but there\'92s more creative ways to get around that. Trust the reader 20% more. Don\'92t hold their hands as much. Wanted more specifics and flaws of Maya. Also, why is Maya like this? Why does she see monsters? Sam wanted it to come around to that at some point. Dig into a little bit more of the why. Why her specifically? Wanted more conflict and higher stakes at the beginning. She wanted the conflict of Maya fighting he monsters, at a younger age, to continue. Maybe try starting with Maya in real time as the art student. It feels like a more palpable conflict. Maybe consider making present day Maya in present tense. So when you go back in time, its clear the you are. Will thought that the going back in time with \'93when Maya was 6\'94 was too much hand holding. On a sentence-to-sentence level the prose had a lot of flourishes. Heavy on the similes. The prose didn\'92t help to visualize the scene better or understand the characters better. They were even getting in the way of the stories some times. Trim some of that to bring the length down. The other characters can also be fleshed out more. She likes the banter between the friends. But the bitchiness can get turned up so we can get a glimpse of the culture of the art school. Telling us exactly what Maya is feeling can sometimes be too much. It\'92s more fun to red as a reader when you\'92re not being told what the character is feeling. (Example: seeing their body movement after an insult). Some details were even unnecessary. Lot of ocean and wave similes, especially int he gallery scene. It was maybe a bit too many, especially because she didn\'92t see how the motif was connected to any broader theme. She was also wondering about the doctor and WHY the doctor? SHe felt like it would come back around but then it dropped off. Maybe that\'92s an original trauma from why she started seeing monsters in the first place. \
Kelsey: Big critique is a fundamental mismatch of stakes. She can see monsters but she\'92s worried about an MFA program? Does tensions didn\'92t feel equal to her at all. Lots of telling not showing. Dialogue is great so maybe use that in lieu of exposition. We don\'92t need more description of the twins but distinct voices would be nice. Also get more of Maya\'92s voice in there. Harrison feels like the antagonist and that is incorrect given the monsters. Kelsey doesn\'92t think we need Harrison at all. Maybe dive into what its like to life with monsters on the daily. That would give the story more meat. We also never get moments of Maya interacting with the monsters directly. Liked the dueling senses of the visual and auditory monsters but she wants the relationship to be clarified a lot more. Maybe say something like \'93but nothing was worse than the whispers.\'94 Include the whispers earlier on in the rolodex of monsters. Both Kelsey and Will didn\'92t understand that the ending was giving a visual representation to the visual monster. Also include more of the parents. They seem like good parents. They would probably be at the exhibition. That would also integrate the past and present Mayas. Kelsey also thought it was a nice relationship she wanted more of. Will also thinks it would be useful to have them so Maya regresses more. Kelsey\'92s suggestion is to integrate them with dialogue. On a sentence level, Kelsey thought the writing was its strongest when it was the simplest. That goes along with what Sam said about the flourishes but also sentence structure. Go with the most straight forward sentence structure possible to make details stand out more. Maya\'92s moral decay would be an interesting sub plot. Could also be a mental health parable. There\'92s high rates of these issues and suicide at these \
Michael: This felt like the beginning of a YA novel where we are priming Maya to do something bad. This is because we hardly see her flaws. It feels suspicious. If you want to keep a reader hooked, you make a character that we root for and see tenable steps towards their goal. Make her stake steps to becoming one of the 5. Right now, she just kind of patiently waits. Maybe she tries sabotaging someone? Maybe she does something bad and that lets to getting what they wanted. Recommends Black Swan for inspiration.\
Scott: Likes the idea about her doing something naughty. Didn\'92t buy the ending. Needs more condition. Show vs tell. The whole story seems limited to Maya\'92s interiority except for the parent\'92s reaction to her running to them. Maybe sticking to just her interiority would be good because that moment felt like we would get everyone\'92s thoughts. Because the story is limited. Maybe give the locations of the monsters (like where in the room they are). What\'92s her escapist drawing like? Scott wanted more of Harrison (anti ditto to Kelsey). First time describing Harrison was so neutral. Everyone actually thought Harrison would be the crush. How does she respond to Harrison after she realizes his true nature. Nit-pick: Why does IFA use North East Bard\'92s college. In the first introduction to the Barovich twins, Scott thought they were different people to Jack and Jade. \
Everyone: Don\'92t get rid of Harrison. He\'92s more open about his toxicity while Maya hides it. But Harrison\'92s cruelty should be more subtle. Sam didn\'92t buy their face off. Jade\'92s dick joke might be too cheap but maybe that\'92s Jade\'92s character. The Secret History would be a good inspiration for the story. Caryn Russel\'92s stories are supernatural adjacent. \'93Vampires in the lemon grove.\'94\
