BSFW Sam Schreiber\'92s Workshop:\
##Andrew Sayre\
Liked treatment of artist mentality. Liked the protagonist delves into others. Also doesn\'92t think its speculative fiction because the monsters don\'92t interact with anything. Won\'92t have much traction in speculative fiction market. Really gets at art school universe. Inner logic of main character is strong. If you try to go more speculative with it, it\'92ll lose its charm. You should, instead, go more impressionist with it. Lean into the expressionistic style. Doesn\'92t have to be a clear mental disorder. If you went more speculative, you would lose the influence that your art is inspired by your demons that haunt the psyche.\
##Blue\
Agrees with Andrew that its a well-written, polished story. It\'92s literary speculative with a better chance of getting published in lit magazines. Art school life was well done. Expected strange art school happenings like parties and messing with different materials. Expected backstories between characters. Doubted they would call the school by its full name every time. More nicknames needed. Liked the arc of discovering how to dispel monsters and then the whispers being the final one. if the horror becomes more physical (exactly what Sam is saying, touch the world), then it would be more SFFH\'85Re: art school: I took it more like this is a typical teenager who thinks it\'92s the end of the world if they don\'92t get into this one school\'85 Of course, getting into the school would be great, but not the end of the world. BUT this did not bother me at all, because it is addressed on the page\'85 they have a whole convo about it\'85It\'92s not easy to get lit magazines to publish stuff like this. It happens but its already hard to publish in these magazines and being speculative only make sit harder. Was also confused at the beginning when she was pointing to a giant creature but the kids called her Monster Maya. Why are they calling her Monster Maya? They could call her Monster Lover or Scary Maya. If the protagonist tis manifesting the monsters, it can be more on the page. Right now, they seem scary but random. If each monster comes from an anxiety, we need a bit more of a hint of that on the page. Andrew dittos this as making it much more impressionistic. Sam says you can use this to make her experience hallucinations that don\'92t fit with schizophrenia.\
##David Far\
Felt the ending was unearned. Didn\'92t understand that the Whispers were another monster. Thought the story wasn\'92t speculative. First section feels like the monsters will be real. Chekhov\'92s gun made David feel like this would be a battle royal against the monsters and then he found himself in a literary magical realism piece. For this reason, the promise of the initial scene felt not-payed-off. It seemed contrived that going to the one art grad school would be the end-all-be-all. Maybe make it a different reason to go there. Maybe there\'92s a specific professor they want to study under there. To make it more speculative, the monsters don\'92t relate enough to her core internal struggle. Check out Fisher King. The way you use \'93Whispers\'94 in the story is almost too colorful a use of language. At the transition point of making whispers monster, state \'93why are there still whispers while there\'92s no one there.\'94 Maybe try to talk about how she\'92s gathering up the sounds into an image. Maybe the little spikes in the whispers help her paint the monster? Another idea is that maybe, at some point, Maya tries telling a friend about the things. It would be a good way for the reader to get a sense if they are real and not like things everyone else has. Also, how is Maya different? If she talks about this all with someone else, she can differentiate herself. Everyone has anxieties, insecurities, etc. Does one monster not like another? This could give each monster more a sense of being. Brainstorm more ideas like this.\
##Katherine Erickson\
Really liked the idea of a visual artist trying to capture sound. Didn\'92t find the result satisfying though. Liked that she didn\'92t like herself related to Harrison. Ditto on not being spec fic. You have to have a change but it didn\'92t land. Maybe add more stuff related to that artist community. Ending felt a little abrupt. Check out Bears Discover Fire! The Ugly Chickens! Etc. One thing sound has that the visual doesn\'92t is directionality. Maybe have her \
##Sam Schreiber\
Dittos that its on the line of spec literary fiction. We are never challenged to wonder if they are real or not. Story plays by spec fit rules. She never wonders if she\'92s just seeing things, just hallucinating. The art world here feels too structured and formalized in terms of showing who gets displayed as getting attention for their work. Recommends DC:AU showcase. The last story features Neil Gaiman\'92s Death and the painter. Let the demons behavior mirror their essence.\
##Brian Shaffer\
Ditto all the positve stuff. Loved the gatorade. Great ideas. Side ditto the genre thing, very borderline speculative. Thought the ending was playing my hand too early. Maybe Monsters could wreak havoc in the physical world, even if only emotionally. In the end, she seems almost too satisfied. Like she doesn\'92t have a care in the world. The big scene where every character is introduced should have more art school hijinks\
